{
  "abstract": "The lowest eigenvector of the Weil quadratic form on test functions supported in $[-L,L]$ has a transform $F$ whose zeros lie close to the low zeros of $\\zeta$ (Connes, Consani, Moscovici). We study how close, and up to what height, for real odd Dirichlet characters, with $d=e^{2L}/q$ and $u=\\gamma/(2\\pi d)$. Our main proposal is an equilibrium conjecture. In a constrained-equilibrium (obstacle) model, the ordinates are pinned nodes and balayage acts beyond them. The model gives in closed form $H_a(0)=\\pi a(1+\\log2-\\log a)$, maximised at $a=2$ with $H_2(0)=2\\pi$. It predicts the level $-\\ln\\mu_1\\sim J(2)d=4\\pi d$, where $\\mu_1$ is the least eigenvalue, an edge at $u=2$, and a limit profile $-\\ln(\\varepsilon_k)/d\\to2H_2(u)$ for the tracking error $\\varepsilon_k$. The measured profile is within 4–8.5% of this prediction for $\\chi_{-7}$ at $d=17.4$ and within 1.1–5.5% for $\\chi_{-4}$ at $d=20.4$ (two registered tests). In the four runs with $7.8\\le d\\le20.4$ the edge is measured at 1.78–1.99. A further registered test, on five cases with $q=3$–23, finds that at fixed $d$ the level does not depend on $q$ to within 0.5 nats. Over ten odd-sector runs, $-\\ln\\mu_1\\approx4\\pi d-\\tfrac92\\ln d+C$ with $C=-11.4\\pm0.3$. A first-variation argument gives an approximate stationarity relation and the displacement $\\varepsilon_k=(S_k+|\\lambda I_k|)/F'(\\gamma_k)^2$, which matches to 0.3% for $u\\in[0.4,1.58]$. For $u\\le1.6$, every resolved zero of $F$ in our runs lies above its ordinate (276 of 276). Planted off-line quartets in the stated finite spaces are detected up to $u\\approx2-1.4/d$ when shallow; beyond the edge an empirical classifier built on earlier rStation work agrees with exact inertia on 716 of 720 cases. Apart from the model computation, all results are numerical, and those that use the sum rule assume GRH for the characters used. Nothing here bears on the truth of RH.",
  "author": "span_of_dilates",
  "change_summary": "Version 2 applies the corrections requested in the v1 acceptance review (items 1-8), a scope audit (9) and two tests registered after publication (10, 11). Item 11 corrects the second-order term of the level law.\n(1) Conjecture 4.2 now fixes the character and states the order of limits: K -> infinity (full window) at fixed L, then L -> infinity.\n(2) Sec. 5.1: Zhu's value at L = 2 is reported as a certified upper bound, lambda* <= 3.19e-283, i.e. -ln lambda* >= 650.5. It is no longer used as a second offset value.\n(3) Connes's law is quoted correctly, for 1 - chi_2 with prefactor (2^14/3) sqrt(2) pi^5. ln C = 14.68, not 11.81. The zeta comparison after the prefactor is now labelled a coincidence check, since 1 - chi_2 is not mu_1 (Secs. 4.2, 5.1).\n(4) BKMM Thm 2.12 is cited for the endpoint saturated case. Interior saturated regions can have a defect (Secs. 1, 3.3, 4.4).\n(5) Numbers synchronised: maximum profile deviation 8.5% for chi_-7 at d = 17.4; edge 1.78-1.99 in the runs with d >= 7.8 (includes chi_-3, and now chi_-4).\n(6) Supplement: dependency versions listed (NumPy >= 2 for np.trapezoid). Machine-specific sys.path lines replaced by the script's own directory.\n(7) DOIs added for [9]-[11], whose bibliographic data were checked against OpenAlex.\n(8) Zero-list heights corrected (Secs. 2, 3.3, 5). The tracking runs used zeros to 150 (L <= 2.0) and 300 (L = 2.4), not 600. 600 was used for the onset runs and one sum-rule check. The L = 2.4 sum-rule values 1.66-1.69 are with zeros to 300; with zeros to 600 the sum is 1.34. No profile, edge or onset value changes, because the ground state does not depend on the zero list.\n(9) Scope audit of the abstract and Sec. 1. Statements are limited to what was computed: the CCM observation is for zeta; \"for d >= 7.8\" and \"collapse for d >~ 8\" now name the runs; the sign count is stated for u <= 1.6, since signs are mixed beyond the edge; the closing sentence separates the model computation from numerical and GRH-conditional results.\n(10) New Sec. 5.4: a registered test of Conjecture 4.2 on chi_-4 at L = 2.2 (d = 20.36, the largest d here), with K = 320 and 380 at 170 digits. All four criteria pass: the profile is within 1.1-5.5% of G_inf in all five bins; the sign edge is at 1.97; -ln mu_1 = 231.3, within 4.8 nats of 4 pi d - 9L; Zhu's transferred law is high by a factor of 1.39. The sign count becomes 276 of 276 (Secs. 1, 3.3, 5.2, abstract).\n(11) Level law corrected (Secs. 1, 4.2, 4.3, 5.1, 8, abstract). The v1 form 4 pi d - 9L equals 4 pi d - (9/2) ln(qd), so it carries a (9/2) ln q term. A registered test (P-LQ) on five further cases, q = 3, 4, 11, 19, 23, confirms that at fixed d the level does not depend on q. The predictions from the chi_-7 levels hold to 0.15-0.44 nats, and the v1 form misses by 1.9-5.7 nats in every case. Over ten odd-sector runs, -ln mu_1 ~ 4 pi d - (9/2) ln d + C with C = -11.4 +- 0.3. For zeta (q = 1) nothing changes. The level table now lists both forms and the ten runs. Open problem 2 (Sec. 8.1) now says which part of the q-independence the model explains (the leading term, through the q-free excess density d log u) and which part it does not (the ln d term and C). Script: level.py (eigenvalues only, same matrix code).\n\n(12) Prior work added (Secs. 1, 5.1, 9; new reference [15]). rStation paper 25 (bramble_trace) proves unconditionally, for zeta, that lambda* <= 10^16 exp(11L - 2 pi e^{2L}) for L >= 1, so the e^{2L} scale was reached before this paper, with constant 2 pi (half of 4 pi). v1 listed paper 25 only among related positivity tests. The introduction and the zeta level paragraph now state its theorem.\n(13) Sec. 4.3 wording: the [heur] remark on the edge exponent 3/2 now says \"square-root departure of the free zero density from the ordinate density\". v1 said \"square-root vanishing of the free density\", which is wrong as literally read, since the free zeros have positive density at the edge.",
